+# Regression audit w1 — bounded handoff
+
+2026-10-01 UTC. Task `rr-gateway-spike-20260930-regression-audit-w1`. This contribution is complete as an audit and replay specification. Engine fixes, operator execution and final adoption acceptance are still pending. Only `sub2api-regression-audit/` is changed; no engine, adapter, fixture, workflow or producer branch was edited.
+
+The existing 77-row ledger remains **58 bounded PASS / 5 FAIL / 14 NOT RUN**. [remaining-matrix.json](remaining-matrix.json) maps all 19 unresolved rows to a concrete observable regression, experiment, acceptance oracle and remediation. It adds three integration gates without changing historical results. [OPERATOR-REPLAY.md](OPERATOR-REPLAY.md) defines a finite replay sequence. No new synthetic result is claimed, and no source-text tests were added.
+
+## Evidence and provenance
+
+Read the existing fork PLAN, spike PLAN/results/REPORT/MIMO-DIAGNOSIS, runtime-lab `run.mjs`, `mock.mjs`, `wire.mjs`, targeted adapter/client/error-fence source and normalized operator receipts. Existing receipts are observations from earlier executions, not fresh runs on producer patches. The source manifest names harness `f05e6a4f451a45681c0bbc7b4b0ced17199ff339`; A02/A03 genuine successes were on `222c6d89900e06e38586100ca2d1cfb978a915a7`. Neither proves the pending integrated build.
+
+Upstream claimed pin is v0.2.11 / `96f4c115c9749078f90cbf210a01d39baf3f53b6`, original image `weishaw/sub2api@sha256:2e3b7fab1e84d2e862cde664d09c1ae84fc897093357cf200cbeb0f64b2f2390`. The staged tree has no verified Git provenance: `sub2api-spike/source-snapshot.json` explicitly says `pin_verified:false`. All five hashes in that snapshot match the corresponding current staged files, but none authenticates every gateway/OAuth file read here. A narrow public raw-source verification was denied by the sandbox domain allowlist. Thus code references below describe the **hashed staged source**, not independently authenticated pinned release code. The ledger records hashes of the inspected source and receipts. Before applying any proposed engine patch or counting new runtime results, the coordinator must verify those exact files against the pin and record any difference. No blind source transplant is proposed.
+
+One supported read-only Git preflight (`git status --short`) failed because the linked Git directory is inaccessible/not a repository in this sandbox. It was not bypassed or retried. No add/commit/push was attempted. A self-contained [scoped.patch](scoped.patch) adds the three handoff files from an absent-directory baseline; the coordinator owns integration.
+
+## E06: deliberate accounting drain, with a flawed Responses cancellation observation
+
+The staged `backend/internal/service/gateway_usage_billing.go:573` and `:583` detach streaming/upstream contexts with `context.WithoutCancel`, returning a no-op release function. `openai_gateway_forward.go:1042` uses the detached context for native Responses. `openai_gateway_response_handling.go:246` explicitly marks client disconnect and continues upstream drain for billing; write/flush failures suppress further downstream writes rather than immediately cancelling the provider request. Native compatible Messages uses `detachStreamUpstreamContext` at `openai_gateway_messages_anthropic_native.go:96`, closes the response body on function exit, and keeps parsing usage after writer failure at `:470`. Its scanner queue is 16 lines; configured data-interval timeout can terminate incomplete usage. These are deliberate accounting choices. Disabling client retries or aborting the broker fetch does not undo them.
+
+There is an important timing weakness in the older evidence:
+
+- `first-20260930-faults-fixed.json`, E06-M: first Messages frame reached the client in 4 ms; after the lab's approximately 1.2 s observation, the synthetic upstream was still open. This supports failure of prompt upstream transport cancellation at that boundary. It does not quantify complete drain or resource release.
+- `first-20260930-remaining.json`, E06-A: upstream finished normally at 6003 ms, while the client first-data cancellation was reported at 6011 ms. `run.mjs:111` cancels on its first downstream data chunk. The tiny initial Responses frames can remain buffered in the engine. This receipt **does not prove six seconds of upstream work after client cancellation**. The earlier E06-A cases with zero upstream requests also do not exercise cancellation.
+- A mock `finish` event means its response was handed to its transport, not that a real provider completed billable inference. Socket close is a local transport observation; remote stop-spend remains provider policy.
+
+The corrected experiment needs independent timestamps for upstream first-frame flush, downstream socket destroy, engine upstream close, mock completion and capacity availability. Send a valid early native frame large enough to cross buffering, flush it, wait for client acknowledgement, then destroy the client while the provider fixture remains blocked. Exercise both protocols, plus broker revoke/expiry, and separately test cancellation before first output. The oracle requires `t_cancel < t_upstream_terminal`; otherwise mark the attempt inconclusive, never PASS. Observe for a fixed bound, then intentionally release the fixture and inspect final usage. Use a second synthetic request to measure slot reuse and simultaneous provider work; a released local slot alone cannot establish remote cancellation.
+
+## Is an opt-in native API-key abort safe?
+
+**Not as a forwarding-only toggle.** No supported abort-on-disconnect setting was found in the inspected gateway context helpers/config. Existing stream interval/keepalive settings control silence, not cancellation policy; a provider producing continuously can keep a drain alive. This conclusion is limited to the inspected tree and requires pin verification.
+
+An account-scoped option could be viable for explicitly configured native API-key providers, defaulting to existing drain behavior and excluding OAuth/other routes. Its minimal review scope would be account extra/config validation, the two native request-context creation paths and stream handlers, result metadata, Responses/Messages usage submission, slot cleanup and the existing usage/billing/reservation boundary. Do not globally change `WithoutCancel`, which also protects billing persistence and cleanup. The existing request builder calls the no-op release immediately after building a request; replacing it with a real cancel without changing lifetime would cancel a healthy request before forwarding.
+
+Current handler code (`openai_gateway_handler.go:859` onwards) submits partial usage for disconnect/error results as well as successful forwarding; detached billing (`gateway_usage_billing.go:565`) protects post-request writes. Inflight reservation release and final debit are separate. Aborting before terminal usage can lose output/reasoning/cache tokens and provider effects. Partial or absent usage must be explicitly marked incomplete/unknown, retained for reconciliation, and excluded from any assertion of exact final debit or zero spend. Never fabricate usage, refund as though no work occurred, or report successful review. Exact accounting is safe only with a provider-supported final usage/reconciliation contract; otherwise expose uncertainty or keep drain. Usage persistence must be idempotent and independent of the cancelled request context.
+
+Minimal *future* behavioral tests, after checking existing tests for overlap:
+
+1. Default policy: upstream remains readable after client cancel and terminal usage is persisted once; healthy SSE bytes/tool IDs are unchanged.
+2. Opt-in Responses and Messages: provider transport closes within the agreed local bound, no second request starts automatically, handler/scanner exits, slot is released once, partial usage remains incomplete and no invented successful terminal appears.
+3. Race cancel vs terminal: one usage row/final debit and one slot release, including terminal received just before downstream failure.
+4. Cancel before usage, late usage persistence, worker queue/persistence failure: no false zero charge, silent refund or retry after uncertain effect. Capture pending reconciliation state.
+5. OAuth/default accounts remain on current policy; disabling the option restores default behavior.
+
+Existing disconnect/drain tests such as `openai_gateway_chat_completions_raw_test.go` concern a different route. Reuse relevant native tests where available; do not duplicate raw-Chat tests and call that native Responses coverage. No engine policy was implemented here.
+
+## E07 / G05: engine memory remains unproved
+
+The 8.39 MB replay completed in 29151 ms with one upstream request and client/control RSS delta 27684864 bytes. The lab explicitly collects response chunks up to 12 MiB, so that RSS is partly observer retention. Engine post-load 60.07 MiB is not peak memory. The 485-request soak proves only the recorded workload, not a streaming memory envelope.
+
+A fixed scanner/channel bound is also insufficient. Inspect full data frames, multiline JSON document assembly, pending frames and output accumulators: staged Responses has a 4 KiB writer, scanner/document scanner, asynchronous queue and `BufferedResponseAccumulator` at `openai_gateway_response_handling.go:344`; native Messages queues 16 lines. Slow writes can retain these structures and connections. Broker `native-errors.mjs` limits individual frames to 2 MiB but temporarily concatenates chunks; a per-frame bound does not bound whole-system memory.
+
+Use actual engine process RSS plus cgroup peak, 100 ms samples, baseline/post-drain, worker/goroutine and socket counts. Run finite 8/32/64 MiB valid streams with a streaming client that discards consumed bytes, both protocols, concurrency 1/5/20. Include comment-only pressure, text deltas, large tool results and multiline/near-limit frames; the old comment fixture misses accumulator growth. Predeclare envelope and wall-time limits (replay plan), count all provider attempts, and verify capacity recovery. A payload-size-dependent rise or retained workers makes the test red. Missing engine telemetry means NOTRUN, not PASS.
+
+## F04 / F05: synthetic OAuth is feasible without an identity
+
+The prior ledger's lack of a live test identity is not a technical blocker to **synthetic** refresh tests. The staged repository has an existing seam: `openai_oauth_service.go:19` defaults to the official endpoint, while same-package tests instantiate `openaiOAuthService{tokenURL: srv.URL}` (`openai_oauth_service_test.go:40`). Service constructors accept the OAuth client interface; `OpenAITokenProvider.SetRefreshAPI` injects the real refresh coordinator/executor. This allows the real form parser, HTTP refresh client, coordinator, durable account repository and Redis locks to participate with fabricated credentials and a local synthetic issuer. No general endpoint override, production login, DNS override or account import is necessary. Do not override global HTTP transport. If a seam is inaccessible across packages, add only a test helper or injected client factory in the appropriate test package, subject to exact-source review.
+
+Existing tests already cover form fields/client IDs, local serialization, held lock, degraded lock, invalid_grant race recovery and cleanup after cancellation. Do not add another stub proving those same branches. The missing boundary is **two independent coordinators/processes with real disposable Redis/PostgreSQL plus actual synthetic HTTP refresh**, followed by gateway scheduling using persisted credentials.
+
+`oauth_refresh_api.go:200` degrades Redis lock errors to process-local locking. `:259` performs refresh after reread. The success CAS branch is Grok-specific; other accounts take `persistAccountCredentials` at `:350`. These are concrete race risks to exercise, not proof that OpenAI loses updates in every case. Force a barrier, rotate one-use R0 to R1, race background and request-path refresh, and race refresh against admin reconnect/disable. Observe issuer count, durable credential version and subsequent synthetic gateway authority. Also cover lock TTL expiry and Redis outage with explicit expected containment; two processes can outlive a lock or lose cross-process serialization.
+
+F05 must exercise genuine synthetic invalid_grant/revoked/401 and successful token rotation with a lost persistence acknowledgement. New work must fail closed or require reconnect after confirmed revocation; a stale failure must not disable a concurrently reauthorized account. A token consumed at issuer followed by uncertain persistence must not trigger automatic same-account refresh retry. Synthetic proof stays separate from F07/F08 live subscription login. No live OAuth proof is claimed.
+
+## Other unresolved boundaries
+
+- **C10:** `customer.mjs:67` creates directly in an authorized group. An upstream create may commit before response/persistence fails; `recovery_required` hides the local binding but leaves remote scheduling authority. Test through actual admin API with a lost acknowledgement, broker admission and upstream sentinel counters. Remediate with a durable operation ID, quarantine group with no keys, verified publication and idempotent reconciliation. Test lost acknowledgements at each promotion step; no automatic create retry and no publication after ambiguous promotion. A fake admin that never persists is insufficient.
+- **D03:** child env allowlisting in `run-client.mjs:14` does not prevent same-UID parent procfs reads. Reuse the synthetic-only procfs reproduction in `sub2api-review` as the negative control, then run it through the exact new UID/PID/mount boundary. Check sibling/control proc environments, inherited FDs, writable config/artifacts and mounts, using only synthetic sentinels. Trusted coordinator performs real-key scans separately. No real parent environment was read here.
+- **G04:** restored counts (accounts 10, groups 10, keys 9, users 10, links 8, migrations 289) prove data restoration only. Start a disposable restored engine behind a broker retaining tombstones, replay a stale capability and its grant, and prove zero provider effects. A new authorized synthetic run must work against restored accounts. Also test lost/corrupt/missing broker ledger failing closed; restoring DB keys cannot recreate broker authority. The old networkless restore script intentionally cannot prove admission.
+- **E08/F01/F03/F06:** measure actual cooldown deadline rather than assuming Retry-After=2 means selectable at 2.3 s; inspect slot cleanup after cancel/crash and actual account disable during an active stream. Document whether existing work finishes; deny new work using revoked authority.
+- **D02/E12:** public error fencing already has coverage. Private engine ops/logs can retain raw upstream diagnostics (`openai_gateway_forward.go:1174`). Scan synthetic sentinels across private logs/ops/crash captures; exported evidence contains only counts. Minimize/redact storage as well as public responses.
+- **A08/H05/F07/F08:** proprietary feature, accounting and live subscription gaps remain distinct. Unsupported feature rejection is acceptable only when honest and before effects. Budget admission/final debit/refund requires actual durable balance observations. Production pool remains untouched; absence of a test identity keeps live subscription lanes NOTRUN.
+
+## Final integration requirements and validation
+
+All three genuine clients must pass the strict financial fixture on the **same exact patched engine image digest and integrated harness SHA**, with native protocols and thinking enabled where supported: Codex 0.159.2 + MiMo `mimo-v2.6-pro`, Codex 0.159.2 + OpenRouter `openai/gpt-4.1`, Claude Code 2.1.285 + MiMo Messages. Record effective provider reasoning mode; do not silently select `none`, bridge protocols or switch models. Thinking history/tool-call association and genuine assistant final answer are required. HTTP200/response.completed/tool stdout alone cannot pass. Preserve all old failed Actions and diagnostic attempts as historical evidence.
+
+Zero automatic retries is a multi-layer invariant: client, broker, engine and refresh coordinator. Staged handler `openai_gateway_handler.go:915` supports same-account retries in pool mode, and forwarding has compatibility recovery retries. Before paid canaries, use a synthetic upstream that records an effect and drops before any downstream semantic bytes, after committed frames, and after a side-effecting tool result. Each uncertain attempt must cause exactly one upstream request, even when an engine might label it precommit/transient. Broker/client retry=0 alone is insufficient. This audit does not claim current engine configuration satisfies that invariant.
+
+Validation performed here: JSON parse; exact set equality against all 19 baseline unresolved IDs; totals 77/58/5/14; source/receipt hash inventory; required experiment fields; patch reconstruction against generated files. These are artifact integrity checks, not regression coverage. Engine/loopback/OAuth/container/live checks are **NOTRUN** here: Go is not installed, Docker/root/live operations are prohibited, public source verification is denied, and producers/operator execution belong to the coordinator. No external check was waited on. Final adoption gates remain open without blocking this bounded audit delivery.
